---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import StatTile from "../../components/StatTile.astro";
import Disclosure from "../../components/Disclosure.astro";
import COSDiagram from "../../components/COSDiagram.astro";

const decisions = [
  {
    label: "Why refusal comes first",
    body: "The gates run before anything gets generated. A German-fluency requirement I can't meet, a seniority mismatch, a hard experience threshold I'm short of — if a posting fails any of them, nothing gets written. No wasted effort, and no application that oversells a fit that isn't there.",
  },
  {
    label: "Why the system isn't allowed to lie",
    body: "Every keyword in a generated CV has to trace back to something real in the master CV. If a posting wants a skill I don't have evidence for, the system either finds honest adjacent evidence or leaves it out. It doesn't invent, and it doesn't get to decide that's someone else's problem.",
  },
  {
    label: "Why a human finishes every application",
    body: "Even on full auto-approve, nothing goes out the door without a manual check. Automation handles the searching and the screening so my attention goes into fewer, better applications — not into rubber-stamping whatever the system produced.",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Apply to fewer jobs, better · Sahil Gaji"
  description="I ran my job search like a transformation project: a real automation pipeline, a working retrospective, and an honest dashboard."
>
  <Header current="work" />
  <main>
    <p class="eyebrow">Career Operating System</p>
    <h1>Apply to fewer jobs, better.</h1>

    <div class="metrics" data-reveal>
      <StatTile
        value="< 30 min"
        label="per application"
        definition="Time from job posting to submitted application, including tailoring, quality checks, and human review."
      />
      <StatTile
        value="2h → 10min"
        label="daily search effort"
        definition="Time spent finding and screening postings each day, before vs. after building the pipeline."
      />
      <StatTile
        value="≥ 75%"
        label="ATS quality gate"
        definition="Minimum keyword-match score a tailored CV must reach before it can ship without a flagged, documented exception."
      />
      <StatTile
        value="~ 9 in 10"
        label="go to the target track"
        definition="Share of applications routed to the aspirational track — PMO, process, digital transformation — rather than the operations/quality track kept as a deliberate parallel option."
      />
    </div>

    <section class="prose">
      <p>
        I moved to Germany for a Master's in Digital Transformation
        Management, and finished it into a job market where my own profile
        worked against me: six years, two countries, six domains, none of
        them individually enough. Job applications felt like a black box —
        no feedback loop, no way to know which lever actually mattered.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a while I believed the fix was more effort: more applications,
        faster turnaround, a slightly sharper cover letter each time. Months
        of quiet rejection later, the real lesson landed — I was optimizing
        the wrong thing. <em>(The full story is becoming its own article.)</em>
      </p>
      <p>
        So I stopped trying to apply harder and built a system to apply
        better: one that screens before it writes, refuses before it
        generates, and never ships anything I haven't reviewed by hand.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="inversion" data-reveal>The system's most important feature is that it refuses to apply.</p>

    <h2>What I built</h2>
    <COSDiagram />

    <div class="decisions">
      {
        decisions.map((d) => (
          <Disclosure label={d.label}>
            <p>{d.body}</p>
          </Disclosure>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <h2>What's happened so far</h2>
    <p class="prose-p">
      Of everything that's cleared the gates, roughly nine in ten applications
      have gone to the track I actually want to work in — PMO, process,
      digital transformation — rather than the operations and quality roles
      I could lean on for near-term traction instead. That fallback track
      exists deliberately, as a parallel lever; it's just not the majority
      of what ships. Whether it actually converts faster is one of the
      things the dashboard below will eventually be able to say.
    </p>
    <p class="prose-p">
      Most shipped applications land in the high-70s to mid-80s ATS match
      band. About two in three clear the 75% gate on the first pass; the
      rest ship only as documented, reasoned exceptions — a referral already
      in hand, a near-textbook fit undercut only by scorer noise on a
      company's own proper nouns — never a silently lowered bar.
    </p>

    <div class="dashboard">
      <p class="dashboard-label">Living dashboard</p>
      <p class="dashboard-body">
        Interview and offer conversion: <strong>collecting data since June 2026, updated quarterly.</strong>
        The system is new enough that this section is mostly still empty —
        which is itself the point. An honest, thin funnel beats a padded one.
      </p>
      <p class="dashboard-stamp">last updated 2026-08-04</p>
    </div>

    <h2>What I'd do differently</h2>
    <div class="retro" data-reveal>
      <p>
        If I were starting again, I would spend less time trying to perfect
        the automation and more time validating whether each improvement
        actually increased interview quality. Early on, I enjoyed building
        the system so much that I sometimes optimized the process before
        proving it solved the right problem. The biggest lesson was that
        better decisions matter more than more automation.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>What's next</h2>
    <p class="prose-p">
      The reject gates currently work off explicit rules — a regex for "C1"
      German, a number for years required. The next version routes edge
      cases (a "fluent German" ask that isn't literally "C1," a years range
      that overlaps the cutoff) to a judgment call instead of a silent
      miss. That's already caused two real errors this system has caught
      and corrected on itself — which is either a bug list or a sign the
      review loop is doing its job.
    </p>

    <h2>Verify this</h2>
    <p class="verify">
      This describes a real, currently running system — not a demo built
      for this page. The application ledger itself stays private (it has
      real companies' names and contact details in it), but the gates, the
      guardrails, and the review discipline are exactly as described here.
    </p>
  </main>
  <Footer lang="en" />
</Base>

<style>
  main {
    max-width: var(--measure-prose);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--space-8) var(--space-3);
  }
  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-1);
  }
  h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 5.5vw, 3rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    font-weight: 400;
    text-wrap: balance;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-6);
  }
  h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    margin: var(--space-8) 0 var(--space-3);
  }

  .metrics {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(11rem, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-8);
  }

  .prose p,
  .prose-p {
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
    max-width: 42rem;
  }

  .inversion {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.2vw, 2rem);
    line-height: 1.3;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
    padding-left: var(--space-4);
    margin: var(--space-8) 0;
    max-width: 44rem;
  }

  .decisions {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  .dashboard {
    border: 1px solid var(--color-line);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: var(--space-3);
    margin: var(--space-4) 0 var(--space-8);
  }
  .dashboard-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
  }
  .dashboard-body {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    font-size: 0.98rem;
  }
  .dashboard-stamp {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .retro {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-line);
    padding-left: var(--space-3);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    max-width: 42rem;
  }
  .retro p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .verify {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    max-width: 42rem;
  }
</style>
